---
// The privacy page, at /privacy: what the hosted playmat collects and keeps,
// for how long, what visitor counting and diagnostics record, which outside
// services are involved, and what it never collects. Sources:
// - the playmat as built (internal/hostauth, internal/usergames,
//   internal/game, internal/cards, internal/telemetry);
// - the idle time from the App Platform definition (lib/facts), not typed in;
// - the help address from internal/notice/notice.json (lib/notice);
// - diagnostics, what they never contain and their retention from the
//   observability runbook, docs/observability.md. Its Retention section is
//   provisional (7 days, to be confirmed by the maintainer); when it
//   changes, change the data-retention figures here too. The browser suite
//   checks the "logs" and "metrics" figures against it;
// - visitor counting from Cloudflare Web Analytics' documentation, checked
//   13 November 2026: developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/data-metrics/dimensions/
//   (country, host, path, referrer, device type, browser, operating system),
//   .../data-metrics/data-origin-and-collection/ (page load timings),
//   .../faq/ (unsampled data kept 7 days, then about a tenth of it, available
//   for six months) and blog.cloudflare.com/free-privacy-first-analytics-for-a-better-web/
//   (no cookies or localStorage, no fingerprinting). The "visits" figure is
//   checked by hand against these pages.
// Laid out like the terms: a docs text column with an On this page card
// (beside it on a wide screen, above the sections on a narrow one).
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { limits, site } from '../lib/facts';
import { notice } from '../lib/notice';

const updated = '21 November 2026';

// The On this page card: each section's id and its heading, in page order.
const toc = [
  { id: 'sign-in', label: 'When you sign in' },
  { id: 'while-playing', label: 'While you play' },
  { id: 'visitor-counting', label: 'Visitor counting' },
  { id: 'diagnostics', label: 'Diagnostics' },
  { id: 'how-long', label: 'How long it is kept' },
  { id: 'outside-services', label: 'Outside services' },
  { id: 'never', label: 'What it never collects' },
  { id: 'removing', label: 'Removing your data' },
  { id: 'contact', label: 'Questions' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What the hosted tcgreplay playmat collects when you sign in and play, what visitor counting and diagnostics record, how long it keeps it, and what it never collects."
>
  <article class="policy privacy prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="updated" data-updated>Last updated {updated}.</p>
    <p>
      This page covers the hosted playmat at <a href={site.publicURL}>{site.publicURL.replace('https://', '')}</a>.
      If you run tcgreplay on your own computer, nothing leaves your machine except the download of the project's
      published card data and the card images your browser loads.
    </p>
    <p>
      The playmat sets no cookies on this website; its only cookies are the two sign-in cookies (below). Cloudflare,
      which sits in front of the site, may set a short-lived bot-protection cookie, <code>__cf_bm</code>. It is not used
      for visitor counting and is not tied to your account. The only things this website and the board load from other
      sites are their typeface, IBM Plex, from Google Fonts (see <a href="#outside-services">outside services</a>), and
      Cloudflare's visitor-counting script (see <a href="#visitor-counting">visitor counting</a>).
    </p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page" data-toc>
      <span class="toc-head">On this page</span>
      <ul>
        {toc.map((t) => <li><a href={`#${t.id}`}>{t.label}</a></li>)}
      </ul>
      <a class="toc-other" href="/terms">Terms of service →</a>
    </nav>

    <section id="sign-in" aria-labelledby="h-sign-in">
      <h2 id="h-sign-in">When you sign in</h2>
      <p>
        You sign in with Google. The playmat asks Google only for your email address
        (the <code>openid</code> and <code>email</code> permissions) and keeps three things:
      </p>
      <ol class="kept">
        <li>your Google account ID, a number Google uses to tell accounts apart;</li>
        <li>your email address;</li>
        <li>whether Google has verified that email address.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        It never asks for or keeps your name, photo or password. Google hands the playmat a token when you sign in,
        which it uses once to confirm who you are and then throws away.
      </p>
      <p>
        The playmat keeps your sign-ins in its memory. The copies it gives your browser (a cookie) and your assistant
        are encrypted, so you stay signed in when the playmat is updated. Two cookies keep you signed in, both hidden
        from scripts:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><code>tcgreplay_session</code>, which keeps the board signed in;</li>
        <li><code>tcgreplay_login_…</code>, which exists only while a Google sign-in is in progress.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The board also remembers seven display settings in your browser (replay speed, autoplay, where the panel sits,
        prize reminders, the interface theme, the mat and whether the key to the action buttons shows). They never leave your browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="while-playing" aria-labelledby="h-while-playing">
      <h2 id="h-while-playing">While you play</h2>
      <p>
        The playmat keeps your current game: the battle log and decklists you import, the moves on the board, its
        branches, the assistant's coaching notes and the opponent's likely list. Each Google account has its own game,
        and only that account can open its board.
      </p>
      <p>
        All of it is held in memory only. It is never written to disk or to any storage service, and it is gone when
        the playmat restarts.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="visitor-counting" aria-labelledby="h-visitor-counting">
      <h2 id="h-visitor-counting">Visitor counting</h2>
      <p>
        The website and the board count visits with <strong>Cloudflare Web Analytics</strong>. For each page view it
        records:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>the page;</li>
        <li>the referrer (the site you came from);</li>
        <li>your country;</li>
        <li>your device: its type (phone, tablet or computer), browser and operating system;</li>
        <li>how quickly the page loaded.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        It sets no cookies and stores nothing in your browser. It is never linked to your Google account, and it is
        never sent your email address, your games or your cards. Blocking it changes nothing about how the playmat
        works.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cloudflare keeps the counts for <span data-retention="visits">six months</span>: the full detail for the first
        7 days, then a sample of about a tenth of it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="diagnostics" aria-labelledby="h-diagnostics">
      <h2 id="h-diagnostics">Diagnostics</h2>
      <p>To keep the playmat running, two kinds of diagnostics are kept:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>DigitalOcean's request and performance metrics</strong>: how many requests the playmat answers, how
          quickly, and with which status codes, and its CPU, memory and restarts. These are totals for the whole
          playmat, not per person.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The playmat's own usage and error records</strong>: sign-ins, refused sign-ins and sign-outs, each
          request your assistant makes (which tool, and which assistant), imports and new games, your first activity
          each day, limits reached, games expiring, accounts removed, attempts to open another player's board, refused
          visits to the admin page, the maintainers' admin checks and actions, and errors, with any of your own text
          removed from the error message.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each record names you only by a <strong>player ID</strong>, a one-way code made from your email address with a
        secret key. It cannot be turned back into your email address. If you ask for help, the maintainer can work out
        your player ID from your email address to find your records.
      </p>
      <p>
        These records never contain your email address or name, Google tokens or the playmat's own tokens and cookies,
        battle logs, decklists or card data.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both are kept in DigitalOcean's Insights service: usage and error records
        for <span data-retention="logs">7 days</span>, and the metrics for <span data-retention="metrics">7 days</span>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="how-long" aria-labelledby="h-how-long">
      <h2 id="h-how-long">How long it is kept</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your game</strong> is dropped after <span data-idle>{limits.idleTimeout}</span> without activity, when
          your last sign-in ends, and whenever the playmat is updated or restarted. To keep a game, ask your assistant for
          its record and save it yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your account details</strong> (ID, email, verified flag) are kept with your sign-ins and dropped
          when your last one ends. Signing out ends the board's sign-in. Your assistant's sign-in ends when its
          connection runs out, up to 30 days after it was last used. Disconnecting tcgreplay in your assistant does not
          tell the playmat, so that record runs out on its own within those 30 days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Board sign-in</strong> lasts up to 30 days. <strong>Your assistant's connection</strong> lasts up to
          30 days and is renewed while you use it; each access it is given lasts one hour. A Google sign-in in progress
          lasts up to 10 minutes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Logs.</strong> The playmat's own records hold no email address;
          see <a href="#diagnostics">diagnostics</a> for what they hold and how long. The hosting providers, DigitalOcean and
          Cloudflare, may keep their own request logs.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="outside-services" aria-labelledby="h-outside-services">
      <h2 id="h-outside-services">Outside services</h2>
      <ul class="services">
        <li><strong>Google</strong>, for signing in.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Google Fonts</strong> serves the typeface the website and the board use (IBM Plex). Your browser
          fetches it from Google, which sees that request and your IP address, as with any visit to a website; no
          cookies are sent, and nothing ties the request to your account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>TCGdex</strong> and <strong>malie.io</strong>, the sources of the card data, which the project builds
          and publishes ahead of time; the playmat reads only that published copy.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Card images</strong>: the playmat fetches them from TCGdex and malie.io and keeps copies in its image
          store on DigitalOcean Spaces. Your browser loads them from there, or from TCGdex or malie.io if a copy is
          missing. The playmat sends nothing about you to TCGdex or malie.io; when your browser loads an image from
          them, they see that request as they would any visit to their site.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>DigitalOcean</strong> hosts the playmat, stores the card data and images (DigitalOcean Spaces) and
          keeps the diagnostics (Insights).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> delivers the playmat's traffic and counts visits (Cloudflare Web Analytics).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your AI assistant</strong> (Claude, ChatGPT or another) sees what you share with it and what the
          playmat tells it, under that assistant's own privacy terms. The playmat tells
          it your <span data-assistant-id>Google account ID (never your email address), so it can tell your accounts apart</span>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="never" aria-labelledby="h-never">
      <h2 id="h-never">What it never collects</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Your password, name or photo.</li>
        <li>Your Google access tokens, after the moment of sign-in.</li>
        <li>Advertising or cross-site tracking data, or anything that identifies you in visitor counts.</li>
        <li>Anything to sell or share: the playmat sells or shares nothing.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="removing" aria-labelledby="h-removing">
      <h2 id="h-removing">Removing your data</h2>
      <p>
        Sign out from the board's menu to end the board's sign-in. Disconnect tcgreplay in your assistant's connector
        settings to stop it using the playmat; the playmat's record of that connection runs out within 30 days. There
        is no stored account to delete: nothing is kept beyond your sign-ins, and the diagnostic records name you only
        by your player ID and run out after the periods above.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="contact" aria-labelledby="h-contact">
      <h2 id="h-contact">Questions</h2>
      <p>Open an issue on <a href={notice.help.url}>the project's {notice.help.label}</a>.</p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The layout, the date and the On this page card are the policy pages'
     shared styles (styles/site.css, .policy). */

  /* What sign-in keeps: one white card, a numbered row each. */
  .kept {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0;
    padding: 0;
    counter-reset: kept;
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--panel);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--line);
  }
  .kept li {
    counter-increment: kept;
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
  }
  .kept li + li { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .kept li::before {
    content: counter(kept, decimal-leading-zero);
    flex: none;
    padding-top: 3px;
    font: 600 var(--fs-12) var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  /* Outside services: one white card, a row each. */
  .services {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--space-3) 0;
    padding: 0;
    border-radius: 12px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: var(--panel);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 1px var(--line);
  }
  .services li { margin: 0; padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4); }
  .services li + li { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .services strong { color: var(--ink); }
</style>
